--TEST--
Core\Db\Schema::fromArray refuses a type outside the vocabulary and a value the form has no place for, rather than carrying either through — `rule:core-classes/schema-is-a-value` and `rule:core-classes/schema-vocabulary-is-closed`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// § 2's vocabulary is closed and there is no raw escape hatch anywhere inside a
// schema value. That is what makes the diff **total** — nothing opaque can be
// in a value, so "this tool does not understand part of your schema" is not a
// state that exists — and it is only true if the refusals land at the door,
// where the caller who wrote the array is still on the stack.

// A vendor's own type name is exactly what the vocabulary does not carry: the
// column type enum is `rule:core-classes/db-column-types`'s type map in the write direction, and a
// spelling outside it is refused rather than passed through to a server that
// might have accepted it.
try {
    Core\Db\Schema::fromArray(["tables" => [["name" => "notes", "columns" => [["name" => "id", "type" => "serial"]]]]]);
    echo "a type outside the vocabulary was accepted\n";
} catch (LogicError $unknown) {
    echo $unknown->message, "\n";
}

// `null` is not a value the form has a place for: a key a schema does not set
// is left out, which is what makes a sparse array and a fully spelled one the
// same schema rather than two.
try {
    Core\Db\Schema::fromArray(["tables" => [["name" => "notes", "columns" => [["name" => "id", "type" => "int64", "default" => null]]]]]);
    echo "a null was accepted\n";
} catch (LogicError $absent) {
    echo $absent->message, "\n";
}

// An identifier is validated and never delimited — the judgement
// `Core\Db::quoteIdentifier` already made for this project, which `rule:security/tainted-qualifier`
// allows only one answer to — so a name that is not a plain identifier is
// refused where it enters the value rather than escaped into safety later.
try {
    Core\Db\Schema::fromArray(["tables" => [["name" => "notes; drop table users", "columns" => [["name" => "id", "type" => "int64"]]]]]);
    echo "an identifier was escaped instead of refused\n";
} catch (LogicError $name) {
    echo $name->message, "\n";
}

// And the refusals are the array's own coherence too, not just its leaves: an
// identity column must be in the primary key, which is SQLite's requirement and
// the other four backends' assumption.
try {
    Core\Db\Schema::fromArray(["tables" => [["name" => "notes", "columns" => [["name" => "id", "type" => "int64", "identity" => true]]]]]);
    echo "an identity column outside the primary key was accepted\n";
} catch (LogicError $identity) {
    echo $identity->message, "\n";
}

// Four refusals leave nothing behind: a schema value is built or it is not, so
// the next array the vocabulary does accept answers its canonical form with no
// trace of what was refused before it.
array<mixed> $accepted = [
    "tables" => [
        ["name" => "notes", "columns" => [["name" => "id", "type" => "int64", "identity" => true]], "primary_key" => ["id"]],
    ],
];
echo Core\Json::encode(Core\Db\Schema::fromArray($accepted)->toArray()), "\n";
--EXPECT--
Core\Db\Schema::fromArray(): `serial` is not one of the column types the vocabulary contains
Core\Db\Schema::fromArray(): the array["tables"][0]["columns"][0]["default"] holds `null`, where a key a schema does not set is left out instead. A schema array holds only strings, integers, floats, booleans and arrays of those — `rule:core-classes/schema-vocabulary-is-closed`'s vocabulary is closed and there is nowhere in it to put anything else
Core\Db\Schema::fromArray(): `notes; drop table users` is not a bare identifier — a letter or `_`, then letters, digits or `_`
Core\Db\Schema::fromArray(): identity column `id` is not in its table's primary key, which SQLite requires and the other four assume
{"tables":[{"name":"notes","columns":[{"name":"id","type":"int64","null":false,"identity":true}],"primary_key":["id"],"unique":[],"indexes":[]}]}
